---
title: "Base64 From Scratch: Six Bits at a Time"
author: Lateralus contributors
date: 2026-11-25
abstract: A walk through stdlib/base64.ltl - how six-bit groups turn three bytes into four characters, why the implementation uses bit operations, and the RFC 4648 vectors that pin it down.
---

# Why Base64 exists

Many text channels only carry text safely. JSON strings, HTTP headers, XML
documents, environment variables, and URL query parameters can all corrupt or
reject arbitrary bytes. Base64 maps arbitrary bytes to 64 printable characters
that those channels can carry unchanged.

The cost is about 33 percent more bytes. The benefit is that the data survives
the trip. Base64 is not encryption and it is not compression. It is only a
re-encoding.

# The core idea: 3 bytes become 4 characters

A byte contains 8 bits. A Base64 character contains 6 bits. The least common
multiple of 8 and 6 is 24, which is exactly three bytes or four characters.
The encoder therefore consumes three bytes at a time:

```
byte 0     byte 1     byte 2
aaaaaabb   bbbbcccc   ccdddddd

sextets:
aaaaaa     bbbbcc     ccdddd     dddddd
```

Each sextet is an index from 0 to 63 into this alphabet:

```lateralus
let CHARS = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/"
```

The last two characters, `+` and `/`, are useful in text but inconvenient in a
URL. RFC 4648 also defines a URL-safe alphabet that replaces them with `-` and
`_`. The URL-safe functions in `stdlib/base64.ltl` perform that mapping.

# Extracting the six-bit groups

The first sextet is the top six bits of byte 0. It is tempting to write this:

```lateralus
let c0 = b0 / 4          // wrong in Lateralus
```

That does not work. In Lateralus, `/` produces a floating-point value. A float
cannot be used as a string index, so the next line fails when indexing `CHARS`.

The integer-safe version uses shifts and masks:

```lateralus
let c0 = b0 >> 2
let c1 = ((b0 & 3) << 4) | (b1 >> 4)
let c2 = ((b1 & 15) << 2) | (b2 >> 6)
let c3 = b2 & 63
```

`>>` discards low bits, `<<` makes room for the next value, `&` masks off bits
that do not belong, and `|` combines the two halves. These four lines are the
core of the encoder.

`ord()` turns a character into its numeric value. It replaces an earlier,
incorrect call to a non-existent `char_code()` function.

# Padding

Most inputs are not a multiple of three bytes. For the final short group, RFC
4648 pads the output with `=` until its length is a multiple of four:

```lateralus
if padding <= 1 {
    result = result + CHARS[c2]
} else {
    result = result + PAD
}

if padding == 0 {
    result = result + CHARS[c3]
} else {
    result = result + PAD
}
```

Two missing input bytes produce two `=` characters. One missing input byte
produces one `=`. The padding carries no data. It tells the decoder how many
bits in the last group are real.

# Decoding

Decoding is the same bit layout read in reverse:

```lateralus
let byte0 = (c0 << 2) | (c1 >> 4)
result = result + chr(byte0)

if has_c2 {
    let byte1 = ((c1 & 15) << 4) | (v2 >> 2)
    result = result + chr(byte1)
}

if has_c3 {
    let byte2 = ((v2 & 3) << 6) | v3
    result = result + chr(byte2)
}
```

`char_index()` maps a Base64 character back to its six-bit value. `chr()` turns
the reconstructed numeric value back into a string character. `decode()`
expects valid, padded input. It is a decoder, not a validator.

# URL-safe Base64

`encode_url()` calls `encode()` and then performs two substitutions:

```lateralus
if c == "+" {
    result = result + "-"
} else if c == "/" {
    result = result + "_"
} else {
    result = result + c
}
```

`decode_url()` reverses the substitution before calling `decode()`. Use the
URL-safe pair for signed tokens, query parameters, filenames, and other places
where `+` and `/` have special meaning. These functions keep the `=` padding;
they do not implement the optional unpadded URL-safe form.

# Validation and length helpers

`is_valid()` checks the structural rules for padded standard Base64:

- The length must be a multiple of four, except for the empty string.
- Padding may appear only at the end.
- Every non-padding character must belong to the standard alphabet.

It does not prove that a string decodes to meaningful data, and it does not
accept the URL-safe alphabet. Validate URL-safe input separately if your
application requires that guarantee.

The length helpers make buffer sizing predictable:

```lateralus
pub fn encoded_length(n: int) -> int {
    return to_int(floor((n + 2) / 3)) * 4
}

pub fn decoded_length(encoded: str) -> int {
    let n = len(encoded)
    let padding = 0
    if n > 0 and encoded[n - 1] == PAD {
        padding = padding + 1
    }
    if n > 1 and encoded[n - 2] == PAD {
        padding = padding + 1
    }
    return (n >> 2) * 3 - padding
}
```

`encoded_length()` deliberately converts the floating-point division result
back to an integer. `decoded_length()` uses a shift because it needs integer
floor division as well.

# Pinned RFC 4648 vectors

Correctness must come from the specification, not from an encoder agreeing
with its own decoder. RFC 4648 section 10 gives these vectors:

| input | Base64 |
|---|---|
| `""` | `""` |
| `"f"` | `Zg==` |
| `"fo"` | `Zm8=` |
| `"foo"` | `Zm9v` |
| `"foob"` | `Zm9vYg==` |
| `"fooba"` | `Zm9vYmE=` |
| `"foobar"` | `Zm9vYmFy` |

The pattern is useful to remember: every extra input byte adds two output
characters until a new three-byte group begins, and padding shrinks as the
final group fills up.

# Running the example

`examples/base64_vectors.ltl` contains the seven RFC vectors, the matching
decode checks, round-trips, URL-safe cases, validation checks, and length
helper checks:

```
$ lateralus run examples/base64_vectors.ltl
=== RFC 4648 base64 encode vectors ===
  ok   encode empty
  ok   encode f
  ...
=== validation ===
  ok   ==== is invalid
=== length helpers ===
  ok   encoded_length(5) == 8
  ok   decoded_length(Zm9vYmFy) == 6

pass=27 fail=0
```

The example inlines the implementation because the Python runner does not yet
resolve `import stdlib.base64`. Keep the inlined block synchronized with the
module. `tests/test_base64_stdlib.py` executes the real module source as well,
so a divergence between the module and its documented behavior is caught.

# Current byte-model limitation

`stdlib/base64.ltl` accepts a `str` and uses `ord()` on each character. It is
therefore safe for byte-valued strings in the range 0 through 255, but it is
not a complete UTF-8 Base64 API. A future revision should encode UTF-8 bytes
before applying the algorithm. Until then, the example intentionally uses
ASCII input.

Also remember the security boundary: Base64 provides neither confidentiality
nor integrity. Use a cipher for secrecy and a MAC or signature for integrity.

# Further reading

- RFC 4648 section 4: the standard alphabet and padding rules.
- RFC 4648 section 5: the URL-safe alphabet.
- `stdlib/hash.ltl`: checksums for non-cryptographic integrity checks.
- `stdlib/chacha20.ltl`: authenticated encryption when secrecy is required.
